From d36e53ad316c86de191135067d86dc3ff9e1426c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karim Baba <karim.sf09@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 12:58:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Start WARDEN from the section that noticed it was down

The empty state said "WARDEN is not running" and left the obvious next
move somewhere else entirely: the Dock, Spotlight, or a login agent that
only fires at boot. It is a button now. The launch goes through
LaunchServices by bundle id rather than a spawned `open`, which the
sandbox would have stopped at /Applications, and which also means the
button works wherever WARDEN is installed.

The spinner ends on the HANDSHAKE, not on the launch: LaunchServices
reports success the moment the process exists, several steps before
there is anything for the section to attach to. The watcher drops to a
250ms poll for the same window, so the globes arrive when WARDEN is
ready rather than up to two seconds later, and a 15s backstop turns
"WARDEN started, but its bridge did not" into a line a person can read
instead of a spinner that never stops.

One thing this cannot do: pass `--hidden`. WARDEN reads that from argv
and would keep its war-room window down, which is what a click in the
notch actually wants. NSWorkspace drops OpenConfiguration.arguments for
a sandboxed caller: the same test binary, signed twice, launches WARDEN
as `warden --hidden` without the sandbox entitlement and as bare
`warden` with it. So the flag is left off rather than passed and
silently ignored, and WARDEN opens its window on this launch the way it
does on any other.
